       "16x16": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/bad0d7258385e7c50bbb31a12cb1ec37?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FGR-3.png",
       "24x24": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/bad0d7258385e7c50bbb31a12cb1ec37?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FGR-3.png",
       "32x32": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/bad0d7258385e7c50bbb31a12cb1ec37?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FGR-3.png",
       "48x48": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/bad0d7258385e7c50bbb31a12cb1ec37?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FGR-3.png"
      },
      "displayName": "Glen Robson",
      "self": "https://fedora-repository.atlassian.net/rest/api/3/user?accountId=557058%3A4bdf8441-c99c-40a3-8b8e-c58dbc2c584f",
      "timeZone": "America/Halifax"
     },
     "body": "<p>I think I have discovered the problem in the fedora 33 source:<br/>\n./fcrepo-server/src/main/java/fedora/server/journal/readerwriter/multicast/rmi/RmiTransport.java<br/>\nSource code was:<br/>\n194     @Override<br/>\n195     public void closeFile() throws JournalException {<br/>\n196         try </p>\n{\n197             super.testStateChange(State.FILE_CLOSED);\n198             parent.writeDocumentTrailer(xmlWriter);\n199             xmlWriter.close();\n201             super.setState(State.FILE_CLOSED);\n202         }\n<p> catch (XMLStreamException e) </p>\n{\n203             throw new JournalException(e);\n204         }<br/>\n205     }<br/>\n<br/>\nWhich meant the RMI process was never calling the remove close function. I changed it to:<br/>\n<br/>\n194     @Override<br/>\n195     public void closeFile() throws JournalException {<br/>\n196         try {\n197             super.testStateChange(State.FILE_CLOSED);\n198             parent.writeDocumentTrailer(xmlWriter);\n199             xmlWriter.close();\n200             writer.close();\n201             super.setState(State.FILE_CLOSED);\n202         } catch (XMLStreamException e) {203             throw new JournalException(e);204         }\n<p> catch (IOException tExcpt) </p>\n{\n205             throw new JournalException(tExcpt);\n206         }\n<p>207     }</p>\n\n<p>i.e. added the writer.close() and it now closes it correctly. I built the source code by downloading the src and running mvn package in the root source dir. I've deployed this rebuilt jar to our Fedora journalling infrastructure and its been working well for the past 2 months. </p>",
     "created": "30/Aug/13 5:17 PM",
